---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---
<Layout pageId="about">
<section class="page-hero">
  <div class="container page-hero__grid">
    <div><span class="eyebrow">About the society</span><h1>What the society does.</h1><p class="page-hero__lead">Oberlin students use the society to find project teammates, prepare 3-2 questions, and organize technical programs.</p></div>
    <div class="page-hero__side"><figure class="club-mark-stage"><img src="assets/images/iglogo-900.webp" width="900" height="900" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" alt="Oberlin 3-2 Engineering Society emblem"></figure></div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="container split">
    <div>
      <span class="eyebrow">Why this club</span>
      <h2>Engineering students are spread across campus.</h2>
      <p>Students interested in engineering may study physics, computer science, mathematics, chemistry, environmental studies, TIMARA, economics, art, or another field. Department boundaries make potential teammates hard to find.</p>
      <p>The society creates a shared contact point for projects, 3-2 planning, and technical events.</p>
    </div>
    <figure class="photo-frame"><img src="assets/images/photos/circuit-boards-green.jpg" alt="A group of green circuit boards on a work surface" width="1400" height="1050" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></figure>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-heading"><div><span class="eyebrow">Operating model</span><h2>How the society turns interest into work.</h2></div><p>Members identify a need, confirm who will own it, and publish details only after the people and resources are in place.</p></div>
    <div class="reading-width">
      <p class="page-hero__lead">The first year is focused on a small number of tasks the founding group can finish and document.</p>
      <p>Members will:</p>
      <ul class="operating-list">
        <li>Recruit members across departments and class years.</li>
        <li>Select projects with a named lead, realistic scope, and documented result.</li>
        <li>Collect student questions and link them to current 3-2 guidance.</li>
        <li>Confirm dates, rooms, and access information before announcing events.</li>
        <li>Record decisions, files, and contacts for the next officer team.</li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>Membership requires no prior technical experience and no commitment to the 3-2 program.</strong></p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section--soft">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-heading"><div><span class="eyebrow">First-year priorities</span><h2>What comes first.</h2></div><p>The founding group is working on membership, advising resources, project selection, and the first event schedule.</p></div>
    <div class="card-grid card-grid--4">
      <article class="card"><h3>Connect students</h3><p>Build relationships across class years, departments, experience levels, and identities.</p></article>
      <article class="card"><h3>Make 3-2 clearer</h3><p>Collect peer questions and point students to current official advising information.</p></article>
      <article class="card"><h3>Finish small projects</h3><p>Choose scopes that fit a semester and document what worked, what failed, and what comes next.</p></article>
      <article class="card"><h3>Establish regular events</h3><p>Run meetups, build sessions, workshops, and conversations with engineers and alumni.</p></article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="container split">
    <div>
      <span class="eyebrow">Who can join</span>
      <ul class="check-list">
        <li>A peer group for students interested in engineering.</li>
        <li>A place to explore technical projects without needing prior experience.</li>
        <li>A way to share questions, resources, and lessons across class years.</li>
        <li>A club for students who want engineering to be part of their Oberlin experience.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div>
      <span class="eyebrow">Academic advising</span>
      <ul class="plain-list">
        <li>The society can help students form better questions and share experience, but it cannot confirm academic requirements or admission.</li>
        <li>Official decisions belong with Oberlin advisers and the partner engineering schools.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section--warm">
  <div class="container split split--center">
    <div><span class="eyebrow">The founding idea</span><h2>Make engineering visible on campus.</h2><p>Kwaku started the society because the interest was already here, but the students were often working separately. The club is meant to make collaborators, project teams, and 3-2 conversations easier to find.</p></div>
    <div><span class="eyebrow">Membership</span><h2>Participation does not require an officer title.</h2><p>Members may contribute through projects, events, documentation, outreach, or regular meetings. The level of involvement can vary by term.</p><a class="button button--primary" href="join">Join the launch list</a></div>
  </div>
</section>
</Layout>
